I see the issue! Look at this CSS rule:

```css
.story-block .scene::before{
  content:'';position:absolute;left:0;top:50%;transform:translateY(-50%);
  width:88px;height:88px;
  background:url(assets/analysis-badge.jpg) no-repeat center;
  background-size:contain;
}
```

And the scene text container uses `padding-left:102px` but the `::before` pseudo-element is `position:absolute` with the background image.

The problem is in the `.story-block .scene` element:


The `::before` pseudo-element is absolutely positioned, so it doesn't actually take up space in the layout—the padding-left on the parent is unnecessary and won't align the text properly with the badge image. positioned with `top:50%; transform:translateY(-50%)` which centers it relative to the `.scene` element. But since the image is 88px tall and might extend beyond the `.scene` element's bounds, it could cover the narrative text below.

Let me trace through the positioning more carefully. The `.scene` has `position:relative`, so the `::before` pseudo-element positioned absolutely within it should stay contained. But if the `.scene` itself is shorter than 88px in height, the image will overflow. I need to look at the actual HTML structure and see what's causing the layout issue—whether it's the scene's insufficient height, the badge positioning, or the avatar images.

Examining the CSS rules... Looking at the HTML structure more carefully, the badge image is a flex item with fixed dimensions, which should work fine. Let me trace through the layout to see where the positioning issue might actually be occurring.

